{"question": "What does the acronym FSRS stand for?", "answer": "Free Spaced Repetition Scheduler.", "tag": "fsrs-model", "extra": "An open-source scheduling algorithm fitted on hundreds of millions of real Anki reviews, unlike SM-2 which was hand-tuned in 1987."}
{"question": "In FSRS, what does a card's stability measure?", "answer": "The number of days until recall probability falls to 90%.", "tag": "fsrs-model", "extra": "Stability is the memory's durability. Every successful review multiplies it; the whole point of scheduling is to grow it as cheaply as possible."}
{"question": "In FSRS, what does a card's difficulty measure?", "answer": "How hard that particular card is for you, on a scale of 1 to 10.", "tag": "fsrs-model", "extra": "Difficulty is intrinsic to the card-and-learner pair, not to you in general. High difficulty slows stability growth, so a difficult card gets shorter intervals forever."}
{"question": "In FSRS, what does retrievability measure?", "answer": "Your predicted probability of recalling the card right now.", "tag": "fsrs-model", "extra": "Distinguish from stability: stability is how long the memory lasts, retrievability is how much of it is left at this moment. Retrievability decays with time; stability does not."}
{"question": "What curve shape does FSRS-4.5 and later use for forgetting?", "answer": "A power function.", "tag": "fsrs-model", "extra": "Earlier versions used an exponential curve, which decays too fast at long intervals. The power curve has a fat tail, matching the observation that very old memories fade slowly."}
{"question": "At what retrievability is a card scheduled by default in FSRS?", "answer": "90%.", "tag": "fsrs-model", "extra": "This is the desired-retention setting. The interval is chosen as the point where the forgetting curve crosses it, so lowering retention lengthens every interval at once."}
{"question": "How many parameters does the FSRS-5 model use?", "answer": "19.", "tag": "fsrs-model", "extra": "FSRS-4.5 used 17; FSRS-5 added two governing same-day repeat reviews. They can be refitted to your own review history with the FSRS optimizer."}
{"question": "Which FSRS input tells the scheduler how much a review taught you?", "answer": "Retrievability at the moment of review.", "tag": "fsrs-model", "extra": "Stability grows most when retrievability is low — reviewing something you nearly forgot is worth far more than reviewing something still fresh. This is the spacing effect, quantified."}
{"question": "What is the spacing effect?", "answer": "Reviews spread over time produce longer-lasting memory than the same reviews massed together.", "tag": "learning-science", "extra": "First measured by Ebbinghaus in 1885 on himself. It is the single most replicated finding in memory research, and the entire justification for spaced repetition software."}
{"question": "What is the testing effect?", "answer": "Retrieving information from memory strengthens it more than re-reading does.", "tag": "learning-science", "extra": "Why a flashcard beats a highlighter: the effort of retrieval is what consolidates the memory. Re-reading feels more productive because it is fluent, which is exactly why it misleads."}
{"question": "Why does re-reading notes feel effective when it usually is not?", "answer": "Fluency is mistaken for knowledge.", "tag": "learning-science", "extra": "Familiarity with the text gets confused with the ability to recall it unaided. Closing the book and trying to reproduce the content exposes the gap immediately."}
{"question": "What is desirable difficulty?", "answer": "Study conditions that slow learning down but improve long-term retention.", "tag": "learning-science", "extra": "Bjork's term. Spacing, interleaving and retrieval practice all make practice feel worse and results better — so learners reliably choose against them."}
{"question": "What is interleaving in study practice?", "answer": "Mixing different topics or problem types within one session.", "tag": "learning-science", "extra": "Contrast with blocking, which drills one type at a time. Interleaving forces you to identify which method applies, not just execute it — the skill an exam actually tests."}
{"question": "What makes a flashcard atomic?", "answer": "It tests exactly one retrievable fact.", "tag": "card-writing", "extra": "The test: if your answer joins two independent facts with 'and', it should be two cards. Compound cards fail as a unit, so you re-drill the half you already knew."}
{"question": "Why must a flashcard question be self-contained?", "answer": "Because you will review it months later without the source in front of you.", "tag": "card-writing", "extra": "'What did he conclude?' is unanswerable in six weeks. Name the entity: 'What did Ebbinghaus conclude about spacing?'"}
{"question": "Why are yes/no flashcards a poor design?", "answer": "A 50% guess rate means passing the card proves nothing.", "tag": "card-writing", "extra": "Rephrase as what/which/why: 'Is haemoglobin a protein?' becomes 'What class of molecule is haemoglobin?'"}
{"question": "What should the 'extra' field of a card contain?", "answer": "Context you read after answering — the why, a common confusion, or a mnemonic.", "tag": "card-writing", "extra": "It must never restate the answer. If nothing useful can be added, that is usually a sign the card is testing trivia."}
{"question": "Why should a long list not become a single flashcard?", "answer": "An all-or-nothing card gets failed forever on the one item you forget.", "tag": "card-writing", "extra": "Replace with per-item cards carrying a discriminating cue: 'Which stage follows X?' rather than 'Name all seven stages.'"}
{"question": "What is a leech card?", "answer": "A card you have forgotten many times over.", "tag": "card-writing", "extra": "Almost always a writing problem, not a memory problem — the card is ambiguous, compound, or has no meaning attached. Rewrite it rather than drilling it harder."}
{"question": "Which FSRS grade should you use when recall was a lucky guess?", "answer": "Again.", "tag": "grading", "extra": "Grading a guess as Good inflates the card's stability, so it returns far too late and is lost. The algorithm is only as honest as its input."}
{"question": "What distinguishes the Hard grade from the Again grade?", "answer": "Hard means you did recall it, with difficulty; Again means you did not recall it.", "tag": "grading", "extra": "Hard still counts as a success and grows stability, just less than Good. Again is a lapse and cuts stability back sharply."}
{"question": "What happens to a card's difficulty when you grade it Easy?", "answer": "It decreases.", "tag": "grading", "extra": "Difficulty also drifts back toward a middle anchor after every review, so a single misgrade cannot permanently distort a card."}
{"question": "Why does FSRS cap post-lapse stability at the card's previous stability?", "answer": "So that forgetting a card can never make it stronger.", "tag": "fsrs-model", "extra": "Added in FSRS-5. Without the cap, the post-lapse formula could occasionally return a higher value than the pre-lapse stability, which is nonsense."}
{"question": "Why do spaced repetition schedulers add random fuzz to intervals?", "answer": "To stop cards learned on the same day from permanently falling due together.", "tag": "scheduling", "extra": "Without fuzz, a deck imported in one sitting returns as one enormous lump on every future review day."}
{"question": "What is the real cost of adding one new card to a deck?", "answer": "Roughly ten future reviews.", "tag": "scheduling", "extra": "Why the new-cards-per-day limit matters more than any other setting: today's 50 new cards are next month's daily hour of reviews."}
{"question": "What does raising the desired-retention setting do to your daily workload?", "answer": "It increases it, because every interval gets shorter.", "tag": "scheduling", "extra": "The relationship is steep near the top: going from 90% to 97% roughly doubles reviews for a seven-point gain. Raise it for an exam, lower it for background reading."}
{"question": "How should you interpret an actual retention well below your target?", "answer": "Intervals are too long — the scheduler's parameters do not fit you.", "tag": "scheduling", "extra": "The fix is to refit the FSRS parameters on your own review history, or raise desired retention. Comparing the two numbers is the main reason to look at the stats screen."}
{"question": "What counts as a mature card in spaced repetition statistics?", "answer": "One with an interval of 21 days or more.", "tag": "scheduling", "extra": "The young/mature split matters because retention on mature cards is the honest measure of long-term learning; young cards flatter your numbers."}
